INT. COUNTY COURTHOUSE - CLOCK TOWER JURY ROOM - DAY

A circular chamber at the top of a stone tower, walls hung with portraits of long-dead judges. A great brass clock mechanism ticks behind a glass panel. Rain beads on the narrow windows. A long oak table, six empty water glasses.

JUDGE AUGUSTINE FAIRWEATHER, sixties, silver-haired and courtly in his robes, pours tea from a thermos. Across from him sits ATTORNEY ROSALIND MACALLISTER, forties, sharp-suited, with a folder held flat in front of her like a shield. Beside her, ATTORNEY THADDEUS PRUITT, fifties, rumpled, taps a pencil in rhythm with the clock.

FAIRWEATHER
Sugar, Miss Macallister?

MACALLISTER
No, thank you, Your Honor.

FAIRWEATHER
Mr. Pruitt?

PRUITT
Two. Please.

FAIRWEATHER
I like these hours before the jury comes up. A man gets to remember he's old.

PRUITT
You're not old, Judge.

FAIRWEATHER
That's what a defence attorney says.

MACALLISTER
We're not here to discuss your age, Judge. We're here about the motion.

FAIRWEATHER
Ah, yes. The motion.

He sips. Sets down the cup. Smiles at them both with enormous warmth.

PRUITT
Your Honor, my client has been very patient.

MACALLISTER
Your client has been very patient for eleven months. Mine has been waiting for six years.

PRUITT
Rosalind, with respect-

MACALLISTER
Thaddeus, with none.

FAIRWEATHER
Children. Children.

He holds up a hand. The clock behind the glass chimes the half hour and everything in the room trembles with it.

FAIRWEATHER (CONT'D)
I have read both briefs. Twice. Once in the afternoon and once at three in the morning, because that's when a man reads honestly.

MACALLISTER
And?

FAIRWEATHER
And I find they're not so different as they pretend to be.

PRUITT
That's... encouraging.

FAIRWEATHER
Isn't it.

Footsteps on the stair. The door swings open and a young woman enters, breathless, a stack of documents hugged to her chest. CLERK PRISCILLA NAKASHIMA, twenty-six, wears a plain grey suit and an expression of dread.

PRISCILLA
Your Honor. Forgive me. This just came from the records office.

FAIRWEATHER
Thank you, Miss Nakashima. Put it here.

She sets the stack in front of him. He opens the top envelope. Reads. His smile does not move, but something behind it does.

MACALLISTER
Judge?

FAIRWEATHER
It seems the original deed was never recorded.

PRUITT
What?

MACALLISTER
That's impossible. I filed it myself.

FAIRWEATHER
You filed a copy, Miss Macallister. The original was delivered to this office in 1987 and signed for by a clerk who is now deceased.

PRISCILLA
It was misfiled. Under the wrong township.

PRUITT
(rising)
Which means the transfer-

MACALLISTER
Never legally occurred.

FAIRWEATHER
Which means, if I'm reading this right, that neither of your clients owns the property in question.

Silence. The clock ticks. Pruitt sits back down, slowly, as if the chair might break. Macallister stares at the judge.

MACALLISTER
Then who does?

FAIRWEATHER
The county.

He picks up his tea. Sips it. Looks at the portraits on the wall, the long line of dead men in black.

FAIRWEATHER (CONT'D)
I suggest we all have a very long think, and then a very short settlement.

PRISCILLA
Shall I fetch the jury, Your Honor?

FAIRWEATHER
No, my dear. I think the jury can go home.
